# Machine-readable citation metadata: the format GitHub reads for its "Cite
# this repository" button, and that reference managers understand directly.
#
# `version` and `date-released` are the two fields here that go stale on their
# own. They describe a release rather than the repository, so they belong to the
# same commit that moves `[workspace.package] version` in `Cargo.toml`, and are
# wrong from the moment one is moved without the other.
#
# There is no `doi` yet, and an absent field is better than a placeholder that
# resolves nowhere. One would come from archiving a release -- Zenodo being the
# usual route, and reached from the GitHub side, which is where the mirror
# already is -- so it is a decision about where the archive lives rather than a
# blank to fill in.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this software, please cite it as below.
title: misah
abstract: >-
  A Rust library for structural geology, with Python bindings through pyo3. It
  intersects planes and triangulated surfaces with digital elevation models,
  fits geological planes to scattered points, solves and inverts the
  Wallace-Bott problem for fault-slip data, estimates kernel densities and
  stress fields over a grid, and compares double-couple focal mechanisms through
  the Kagan angle.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Alberti
    given-names: Mauro
    email: alberti.m65@gmail.com
version: 0.2.0-alpha.2
date-released: "2026-09-09"
license: GPL-3.0-or-later
repository-code: https://gitlab.com/mauroalberti/misah
url: https://gitlab.com/mauroalberti/misah
keywords:
  - structural geology
  - geospatial
  - digital elevation model
  - fault-slip inversion
  - focal mechanism
  - Rust
  - Python
